/*! \mainpage SLiQ-MoCS
  SLiQ-MoCS (Simple Lightweight Monte-Carlo Simulator) aims to provide a simple
  implementation to simulating quantum spin systems and their interaction.

  The later is documented using Doxygen to avoid any misunderstanding in
  code design, and provide the user a simple solution to using the
  program.

  In the aim of running a Quantum Monte-Carlo simulation, several components are necessary. We choose to separate these in several classes under a similar global namespace for the project, qmc.

  The reader can refer to the project paper in addition to browsing this
  documentation, as most of the theoretical background related to QMC
  simulations is explained there.

  These afore-mentionned code components are:
  - A \ref code_mcstep "user interface" to the simulation. We use \ref
    qmc::MCStep for these purposes.
  - A \ref code_ssedata "storage for SSE configuration data".
  + Spin and operator states are managed by \ref qmc::SSEData "SSEData"
  + Observables, needed for measurements, are inherited from \ref
  qmc::Observable "Observable".
  - A \ref code_lattice "lattice", containing structural information about the
  system. This task is handled by \ref qmc::Lattice "Lattice" and its children.
 */

